    id: "4",
    slug: "introducing-mahir-screener",
    title: "Introducing MAHIR Screener",
    date: "2026-06-01",
    excerpt: "Market intelligence built for the everyday investor.",
    readTime: "12 min read",
    content:
      '<p class="lead" style="font-size: 1.25rem; color: #4b5563; font-weight: 500; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Market intelligence built for the everyday investor.</p><p>MAHIR Screener is our answer to a persistent problem: retail investors rarely have access to the same screening tools and data sets that institutions take for granted. We built this product to level the playing field, giving you the ability to evaluate companies using the same rigorous framework we apply in our advisory practice.</p><h2>What Makes a Screener Valuable</h2><p>A good screener does more than filter by market cap or PE ratio. It lets you apply consistent, research-driven criteria across hundreds of stocks — looking at profitability, debt levels, cash flow quality, and governance track records. The goal is not to find a magic formula, but to narrow your focus to companies that deserve deeper analysis.</p><p>Most screeners available to retail investors are either too simplistic, offering basic financial ratios with no context, or too complex, overwhelming users with data points that require professional expertise to interpret. MAHIR Screener occupies the middle ground — it provides institutional-grade data presented in a way that is accessible to informed individual investors.</p><h2>Features That Matter</h2><p>MAHIR Screener includes pre-built filters for quality, value, and growth, along with the ability to save and compare screens. Every filter is derived from the same research methodology we use for client recommendations, ensuring consistency between the tool and the advice.</p><p>The quality filter examines return on equity, return on capital employed, and the consistency of earnings growth over multiple years. The value filter looks at price-to-earnings, price-to-book, and enterprise value-to-EBITDA ratios relative to historical averages and sector peers. The growth filter tracks revenue and profit growth rates, margin expansion, and the sustainability of growth trajectories.</p><h2>Building Your Own Research Process</h2><p>One of the most powerful features of MAHIR Screener is the ability to create custom screens and save them for ongoing monitoring. You can set up a screen that identifies companies meeting your specific criteria and receive alerts when new data changes the picture. This transforms the screener from a one-time lookup tool into an ongoing research assistant.</p><p>We also provide sector-specific screening templates that highlight the metrics most relevant to different industries. For banks, this includes net interest margins and asset quality measures. For manufacturing companies, it focuses on capacity utilization and working capital efficiency. These sector-aware templates help you compare apples to apples when evaluating companies across different segments of the economy.</p><h2>Data Quality and Timeliness</h2><p>The screener pulls data directly from regulatory filings and verified sources, with updates reflecting the latest quarterly results. We apply consistent normalization across companies to ensure that comparisons are meaningful even when accounting policies differ. Every data point is traceable to its source, allowing you to verify the information independently.</p><p>Our research team manually reviews flagged anomalies before they appear in the screener, adding a layer of quality control that purely algorithmic tools cannot replicate. When the data tells a story that seems inconsistent with the headline numbers, we investigate before presenting the information to users.</p><h2>Designed for Learning</h2><p>MAHIR Screener is not just a tool — it is an educational resource. Each screening criterion includes a brief explanation of why it matters and how to interpret the results. We believe that the process of screening itself teaches investors to think analytically about business quality, valuation, and risk.</p><p>Over time, using the screener regularly helps you develop intuition about what good numbers look like and, equally importantly, what red flags look like. This pattern recognition is one of the most valuable skills an investor can develop, and it comes only through practice and exposure to a wide range of companies and business models.</p>',
